From 176a6dd34d313e167d0c12335e019032280add2a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: HuskerMinion <28571744+HuskerMinion@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/40] soc: realtek: Add the RTD129x RBUS erratum lock and the
 audio CPU RPC

RTD1293/RTD1295/RTD1296 up to revision A01 have a hardware erratum: an
RBUS register access at offsets 0x800-0x9ff within any 4 KiB block that
overlaps eMMC controller activity can corrupt the eMMC transfer. The
Realtek vendor kernels ("rtd129x_lockapi") serialize all such accesses
and the eMMC command submission with one global spinlock taken with
interrupts disabled, plus a short NOP settle delay after locking and
before unlocking.

Add a minimal shared implementation of that lock together with
readl/writel helpers that only take it for affected addresses, so that
the PCIe, eMMC and other RBUS drivers can share it. The erratum depends
only on the low 12 bits of the address, which ioremap() preserves, so
the helpers test the virtual address directly.

RTD129X_RBUS_LOCK_DEBUG (off by default) records the holder of the
lock, warns once if the lock is held for more than 1 ms, and reports
the holder (CPU, caller and hold time) with a stack dump once when a
CPU has spun on the lock for more than 100 ms.

Also add the transport to the audio CPU (ACPU) firmware, as the Realtek
vendor kernels implement it (BSP 4.9 common/rpc, QNAP 4.2.8
rtd129x/rpc; both agree on layout and protocol):

- Initialize the poll/intr/kern ring records at 0x1ffe000 (little
  endian, ACPU addresses 0xa0000000 | phys; big-endian messages).
- Ring the doorbell (SB2 0x104 = RPC_INT_SA | WRITE_1) with the vendor
  notify handshake in vo_int_sync, and announce the SCPU with
  audio_rpc_flag = 0xffffffff.
- Kernel RPC to the audio firmware (program 98 on the kern rings), one
  call at a time, reply matched by task ID, 5 s timeout.
- Serve the firmware's remote allocation requests (program 98 on the
  intr rings) from a reserved heap below 512 MiB, replying with the
  KSEG0 address as BSP 4.9 does.
- Process the ACPU-to-SCPU rings from the interrupt thread (GIC SPI
  33), from a waiting caller and from a slow poll (poll_ms, 100 ms by
  default), so a silent interrupt does not stall anything.

The ACPU is not coherent with the main CPUs, and both sides write the
ring records and the words of the common area, so both regions must be
no-map and are mapped write-combined (normal non-cacheable). A write
then only touches its own word, with no cacheable alias that could
write back stale neighbors. Explicit barriers order the common area
accesses: a read is not done ahead of earlier reads, and a write is
complete before a following doorbell or poll.

The firmware has no failure path for a remote allocation: it turns a 0
reply into 0x80000000 and takes an exception in RemoteMallocHandler,
after which no RPC is answered. The heap therefore has to be sized
generously in the device tree. Track the bytes in use, the peak and the
block count, show them in sysfs (acpu_heap: "used peak size blocks"),
and say in the failure message that the firmware will crash.
